    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35674</id>
    <title>Can We Build More Effective Place-Based Policies? Theory and Evidence</title>
    <updated>2026-08-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Matthew Freedman &amp; David Neumark</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Amid renewed policy interest, the academic literature on place-based programs has expanded dramatically in recent years. In this essay, we review recent theoretical and empirical work in an effort to assess when and why place-based job creation policies succeed or fail. Drawing on theory, we describe key dimensions on which place-based programs vary and discuss evidence on the consequences of different design choices. We conclude with several lessons for the next generation of place-based programs.</content>
